  "_title": "Memory — sixteen places to put a number",
  "_description": "The counter from C1 now drives the ADDRESS pins of a 74LS189, a 16-by-4-bit RAM. Yellow LEDs show which address the machine is looking at; green ones show what is stored there. Set the data switches, pulse WRITE, then clock to the next address and do it again — you are loading a program by hand, which is exactly how the first machines were programmed. WATCH OUT: the 74LS189 has INVERTED outputs. Store 5 and the LEDs read 10. That is the real chip, not a mistake, and it is why SAP-1 builds put a hex inverter after the RAM.",
  "_category": "computer",
  "_difficulty": 4,
  "_stage": "C2"
}
